WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:02.900
The Shadow Lace Transfer Tool,

2
00:00:02.930 --> 00:00:06.440
also known as Jaws,
is a handy device for transferring

3
00:00:06.470 --> 00:00:10.090
stitches between the ribber
and the main bed.

4
00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:11.500
It was originally designed

5
00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:16.340
for Silver  / Singer / Studio machines
for a technique called shadow lace.

6
00:00:16.370 --> 00:00:18.700
Most knitters use Jaws when transferring

7
00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.940
a large number of stitches between
the beds, instead of just using a double

8
00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:27.100
eyed needle and transferring
stitches one at a time.

9
00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:32.980
Before working with the tool, let's
review how we transfer stitches manually.

10
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.780
Using the double eyed needle,
we position it opposite the ribber

11
00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:41.900
and insert the hook of the needle
in the hole of the tool.

12
00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:47.060
Pull up the needle and put
the stitch behind the latch.

13
00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:52.320
Push down on the needle to slip
the stitch onto the tool.

14
00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:56.060
Then slip the stitch
to the other end of the tool.

15
00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:58.940
Position the eye of the tool over the hook

16
00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:03.560
of the target needle
and slip the stitch off.

17
00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:10.980
And we can do the same thing
by using two transfer tools.

18
00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:16.100
Position the eyes of the first tool
over the needles to be transferred.

19
00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:19.740
Pull up to get the stitches
behind the latches.

20
00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:24.240
Push down to transfer
the stitches to the tool.

21
00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:27.290
Insert the prongs of a second tool

22
00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:35.260
in the stitches and transfer
the stitches to the second tool.

23
00:01:35.290 --> 00:01:37.980
Set the first tool aside.

24
00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:40.700
Slip the eyes of the tool over the hooks

25
00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:46.140
of the target needles and slip
the stitches onto the needles.

26
00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:52.240
Keeping these visuals in mind,
try transferring with Jaws.

27
00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:54.740
Each side of the tool is numbered.

28
00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:57.380
There's a number one and a number two.

29
00:01:57.410 --> 00:02:00.820
Some jaws have two colors.

30
00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:05.980
Start by making sure that the latches
of the needles on both beds are open.

31
00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:11.300
Position the holes of side one over
the needles to be transferred.

32
00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:16.440
Think of this as positioning
that double-eyed needle.

33
00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:18.700
The way Jaws is constructed.

34
00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:21.020
We can peek down between the sections

35
00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:24.780
of the tool,
watch and confirm that the eyes are lining

36
00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:28.780
up with the needles and that
stitches are transferring.

37
00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:35.960
Pull up to move the stitches behind
the latches, push down

38
00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:45.520
and rotate the tool so you can
transfer the stitches to the tool.

39
00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:55.960
Here you can see the stitches are
no longer on the ribber needles.

40
00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:03.980
But I missed one, so I'll just pick that
stitch up and transfer it to the tool.

41
00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:07.460
Here's where that name Jaws comes in.

42
00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:13.560
Close the tool and transfer
the stitches to side 2.

43
00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:29.440
Shift the tool and position the eyes
of side two over the main bed needles.

44
00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:45.440
Rotate the tool and pop those
stitches onto the new needles.

45
00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:50.820
Keep in mind the process is like using
the double-eyed needle, but with Jaws,

46
00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:53.400
you're transferring a number
of stitches at a time.

